[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Core Java: String Methods - Substring, Replace, Split, etc."},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Strings are a fundamental data type in Java, representing sequences of characters. The `String` class in Java provides a rich set of methods for manipulating strings. This tutorial will cover some of the most commonly used string methods, including `substring()`, `replace()`, `split()`, and others, with detailed explanations and examples."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. The `substring()` Method"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `substring()` method extracts a portion of a string. It comes in two variations:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`substring(int beginIndex)`: Returns a new string that is a substring of this string, starting at the specified index and extending to the end of the string."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`substring(int beginIndex, int endIndex)`: Returns a new string that is a substring of this string, starting at the specified `beginIndex` and extending to the character at index `endIndex - 1`. Note that the character at `endIndex` is *not* included."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"public class SubstringExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "Hello, World!";

        // Using substring(int beginIndex)
        String sub1 = str.substring(7); // Starts at index 7 (W)
        System.out.println("Substring from index 7: " + sub1); // Output: World!

        // Using substring(int beginIndex, int endIndex)
        String sub2 = str.substring(0, 5); // Starts at index 0 (H), ends before index 5 (,) 
        System.out.println("Substring from index 0 to 5: " + sub2); // Output: Hello
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Important considerations for `substring()`:"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `beginIndex` is inclusive, while the `endIndex` is exclusive."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If `beginIndex` is negative or greater than the length of the string, an `IndexOutOfBoundsException` is thrown."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If `endIndex` is less than `beginIndex`, an `IndexOutOfBoundsException` is thrown."},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"2. The `replace()` Method"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `replace()` method replaces all occurrences of a specified character or sequence of characters with another character or sequence of characters. It has two main variations:"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`replace(char oldChar, char newChar)`: Replaces all occurrences of `oldChar` with `newChar`."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`replace(CharSequence target, CharSequence replacement)`: Replaces all occurrences of the `target` sequence with the `replacement` sequence. `CharSequence` is an interface implemented by `String`, so you can use strings directly."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"public class ReplaceExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "Hello, World!";

        // Replacing a character
        String replacedChar = str.replace('o', '0');
        System.out.println("Replaced 'o' with '0': " + replacedChar); // Output: Hell0, W0rld!

        // Replacing a sequence of characters
        String replacedSequence = str.replace("World", "Java");
        System.out.println("Replaced 'World' with 'Java': " + replacedSequence); // Output: Hello, Java!
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `replace()` method returns a *new* string with the replacements made. The original string remains unchanged because strings in Java are immutable."},{"order":18,"type":"heading","text":"3. The `split()` Method"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `split()` method divides a string into an array of substrings based on a specified delimiter (separator). It has two variations:"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`split(String regex)`: Splits the string around matches of the given regular expression."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`split(String regex, int limit)`: Splits the string around matches of the given regular expression. The `limit` parameter controls the number of substrings returned. If `limit` is positive, the array will have at most `limit` elements. If `limit` is negative, the array will contain all substrings. If `limit` is zero, the array will contain all substrings, and trailing empty strings are discarded."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"public class SplitExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = "apple,banana,orange";

        // Splitting by comma
        String[] fruits = str.split(",");
        System.out.println("Splitting by comma:");
        for (String fruit : fruits) {
            System.out.println(fruit);
        }
        // Output:
        // apple
        // banana
        // orange

        String str2 = "one two three four five";
        String[] words = str2.split(" ", 3);
        System.out.println("Splitting by space with limit 3:");
        for (String word : words) {
            System.out.println(word);
        }

        //Output:
        //one
        //two
        //three four five
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Important considerations for `split()`:"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The delimiter is a regular expression, so special characters (e.g., `.`, `*`, `+`, `?`, `|`, `^`, `$`, `(`, `)`, `[`, `]`, `{`, `}`, `\`) need to be escaped with a backslash (`\`)."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"If the delimiter is not found in the string, the array will contain only the original string."},{"order":26,"type":"heading","text":"4. Other Useful String Methods"},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"4.1 `trim()`"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `trim()` method removes leading and trailing whitespace from a string."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"String str = "   Hello, World!   ";
String trimmedStr = str.trim();
System.out.println(trimmedStr); // Output: Hello, World!"},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"4.2 `toLowerCase()` and `toUpperCase()`"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"These methods convert a string to lowercase or uppercase, respectively."},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"String str = "Hello, World!";
String lowerCaseStr = str.toLowerCase();
String upperCaseStr = str.toUpperCase();
System.out.println(lowerCaseStr); // Output: hello, world!
System.out.println(upperCaseStr); // Output: HELLO, WORLD!"},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"4.3 `length()`"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `length()` method returns the length of the string (number of characters)."},{"order":35,"type":"code","text":"String str = "Hello, World!";
int length = str.length();
System.out.println(length); // Output: 13"},{"order":36,"type":"subheading","text":"4.4 `charAt(int index)`"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `charAt()` method returns the character at the specified index."},{"order":38,"type":"code","text":"String str = "Hello";
char ch = str.charAt(0);
System.out.println(ch); // Output: H"},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"4.5 `indexOf()` and `lastIndexOf()`"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `indexOf()` method returns the index of the first occurrence of a specified character or substring. The `lastIndexOf()` method returns the index of the last occurrence."},{"order":41,"type":"code","text":"String str = "Hello, World! Hello!";
int firstIndex = str.indexOf("Hello");
int lastIndex = str.lastIndexOf("Hello");
System.out.println("First index of Hello: " + firstIndex); // Output: First index of Hello: 0
System.out.println("Last index of Hello: " + lastIndex); // Output: Last index of Hello: 14"},{"order":42,"type":"heading","text":"5. Conclusion"},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `String` class in Java provides a powerful set of methods for manipulating strings. Understanding and utilizing these methods effectively is crucial for any Java developer. This tutorial covered some of the most commonly used methods, including `substring()`, `replace()`, `split()`, `trim()`, `toLowerCase()`, `toUpperCase()`, `length()`, `charAt()`, `indexOf()`, and `lastIndexOf()`. Remember that strings are immutable, so most of these methods return new strings rather than modifying the original string."}]
